import { FilesystemReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> FilesystemReaderCopy = FilesystemReaderCopy {
  summary: "Read a whole file as a String using strict UTF-8, distinguishing access problems from decoding problems.",
  taskTitle: "Read back a report containing Japanese text",
  purpose: "Read café and こんにちは from report.txt as the original String. The first part of the example creates known content, so you do not need to prepare a separate fixture just to try the read.",
  setup: "The imported fs handles files, paths handles path values, and effects composes executable work. effect fn report directory: paths.Path -> String defines work taking directory and producing a String when run. with FileSystem is its service requirement; fails ReportError is its ordinary failure type. Within do, <- receives a result and _ <- waits for completion without keeping one.",
  after: [
    "paths.child \"report.txt\" directory returns Left for a problem or Right carrying a Path. Passing it through |> to effects.fromEither creates work that succeeds or fails accordingly. file <- names the successful Path. The read destination is not an arbitrary String.",
    "let text defines an immutable value containing café and こんにちは separated by a newline, and CreateNew writes that content. The final fs.readTextUtf8 file reads it. Because this is the final operation in do, its successful String becomes report’s result. To use that content earlier in a block, place the same operation after <- and give its result a name.",
    "ReportError defines this example’s failure shapes. PathFailure retains a path problem, WriteFailure a write problem, and ReadFailure a read problem. effects.mapError wraps each accordingly, and deriving Show enables display. The original read problem remains inside ReadFailure.",
    "In main, withTemporaryDirectory supplies a dedicated workspace and removes it after report finishes. effects.attempt turns success into Right text or ordinary failure into Left problem; match chooses message. println displays the content read back, followed by a final newline."
  ],
  details: [
    "The call returns an Effect, a value describing work. Opening the file happens when that work executes, not when it is constructed. The successful String holds the entire content, so large files require correspondingly more memory. Success waits until the file is closed; a close failure is also an ordinary failure.",
    "The failure type FileTextError has two shapes. FileAccessFailure contains a FileSystemError, whose kind is FileNotFound for a missing file. FileUtf8Failure contains a UTF-8 decoding problem. Invalid bytes are not replaced with replacement characters and reported as success.",
    "The checked byte sequence [65, 195, 40, 66] fails with InvalidUtf8 at offset: 1. This is a zero-based byte position. An empty file produces an empty String, and a leading UTF-8 BOM is retained as U+FEFF. A BOM is an encoding marker sometimes placed at the start of a file.",
    "Checking existence first does not guarantee that a subsequent read succeeds. Handle readTextUtf8’s result. If you supplied text where a Path is required, repair it by checking the text with paths.parse and passing the Path received in Right."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS can read bytes with readFile and decode strict UTF-8 with TextDecoder. fatal: true rejects malformed UTF-8; ignoreBOM: true retains a BOM as a character, matching this API.",
  comparisonAfter: "readTextUtf8 combines this read behavior in one operation and distinguishes access from decoding problems with FileTextError. Node’s default string read replaces malformed bytes, so it is not the same decoding contract."
}
